'''Bonasa, Bonasia''' (bō-nā′sä̤, -si-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL. Cf. ''bonasus''.] A genus of gallinaceous birds, of the family ''Tetraonidæ'', containing especially ''B. betulina'', the hazel-grouse of Europe, and ''B. umbella'', the ruffed grouse, pheasant, or partridge of North America. {{smaller|They have a ruffle of}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Ruffed Grouse (''Bonasa umbella'').}}}}
{{smaller|feathers on each side of the neck, a broad fan-shaped tail, partly feathered shanks, and a small crest. They are woodland birds, noted for their habit of drumming, whence probably their name, the noise being likened to the bellowing of a bull.}}

