'''3.''' A binding or uniting power or influence; cause of union; link of connection; a uniting tie: as, the ''bonds'' of affection.
{{smaller block|<poem>Farewell, thou worthy man! There were two ''bonds''
That tied our loves, a brother and a king.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid’s Tragedy, v. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|There is a strong ''bond'' of affection between us and our parents. {{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, ii. 14.}}}}
{{smaller block|I have struggled through much discouragement . . . for a people with whom I have no tie but the common ''bond'' of mankind. {{right|''Burke'', To Sir H. Langrishe.}}}}

'''4.''' Something that constrains the mind or will; obligation; duty.
{{smaller block|<poem>I love your majesty
According to my ''bond'', nor more nor less.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Sir Aylmer, reddening from the storm within,
Then broke all ''bonds'' of courtesy.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Aylmer’s Field.}}}}

'''5.''' An agreement or engagement; a covenant between two or more persons.
{{smaller block|I will bring you into the ''bond'' of the covenant.
{{right|Ezek. xx. 37.}}}}
{{smaller block|A ''bond'' offensive and defensive.
{{right|''Sir J. Melvil'' (1610), Mem., p. 12.}}}}

'''6.''' [< D. ''bond'', league.] A league or confederation: used of the Dutch-speaking populations of southern Africa.—'''7.''' In ''law'', an instrument under seal by which the maker binds himself, and usually also his heirs, executors, and administrators (or, if a corporation, their successors), to do or not to do a specified act.
{{smaller|If it is merely a promise to pay a certain sum on or before a future day appointed, it is called a ''single bond''. But the usual form is for the obligor to bind himself, his exec-}}

